    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_5d12d51390716e9082913056190a4c2000acf0962360cabd110b4d21aa69ef56",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The span supports only a conditional complement mechanism: if Robin's inequality is established for the relevant 5-free range while its finite exceptions are separately handled, then every remaining case lies in the complementary class of integers divisible by p^5 for some prime p. As extracted, Theorem 5 cannot be fixed safely: its claim that all 5-free integers satisfy the inequality conflicts with the same span's statement that the known exceptions through 5040 are 5-free.",
